@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' Destination diagram — prose from internal/runner/{runner,pids}.go lands here (batch 6).
title Hardened run argv — one docker-run builder, one pids ceiling

node "internal/runner — pure data, no execution" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "Config\nname · user · mounts · env\nworkdir · entrypoint · image · command" as CFG <<app>>
  component "DockerRunArgs(Config)\n{run, flags…, image, command…}" as ARGV <<app>>
  component "hardeningStatic\ncap-drop=ALL\nno-new-privileges" as HARD <<app>>
  component "Hardening(pids)\nbaseline + --pids-limit" as HFN <<app>>
}

node "Pids capacity — internal/runner/pids.go" as PIDS <<host>> {
  component "DetectHost(preferImages…)\nCPUs · PidMax" as DETECT <<app>>
  component "HostCeiling(HostInfo)" as CEIL <<app>>
  component "MinPidsLimit(browser)\ntier floor" as FLOOR <<app>>
  component "ParsePidsOverride(s)" as OVR <<app>>
  component "EnsurePidsCapability\nhost meets the tier floor?" as ENSURE <<app>>
  component "ResolvePidsLimit\nalways a number, never unlimited" as RESOLVE <<app>>
}

component "IsBrowserImage(image)\npicks the tier" as TIER <<app>>
database "Linux host /proc\nkernel.pid_max" as PROC <<db>>
cloud "Docker engine VM\nmacOS · Windows · Colima" as VM <<cloud>>
component "ErrInsufficientPidsCapability\nhost below the tier floor" as INSUF <<error>>
component "Caller executes the argv\ncmd/proveo · egress exec path" as CALLER <<host>>

CFG ARROW_MAIN ARGV
ARGV ARROW_MAIN HFN : always, never optional
HFN ARROW_MAIN HARD
ARGV ARROW_MAIN RESOLVE : ""PidsLimit <= 0"" — auto-resolve
TIER ARROW_MAIN RESOLVE
TIER ARROW_MAIN FLOOR
DETECT ARROW_MAIN PROC : Linux
DETECT ARROW_CLOUD VM : no /proc — probe a local image
DETECT ARROW_MAIN CEIL
CEIL ARROW_MAIN RESOLVE
OVR ARROW_OPTIONAL RESOLVE : clamped, never trusted raw
ENSURE ARROW_ERROR INSUF : refuse before a container exists
ARGV ARROW_MAIN CALLER

note bottom of VM
  **macOS and Windows have no host ""/proc"", so the ceiling is read
  from inside the engine.** The number that matters for
  ""--pids-limit"" is the Linux VM's ""kernel\.pid_max"", and the only
  way to read it is from a container: ""DetectHost"" runs
  ""cat /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max"" in an //already local// image with
  ""--pull=never"", so a capacity probe never triggers a network fetch.

  **That makes the probe order-dependent for the caller.**
  ""cmd/proveo"" preflights the agent image //before// detection
  precisely so the probe has something to run; the full sidecar
  preflight still happens later, once the plan is assembled.
end note
note right of HARD
  **The hardening baseline is not optional and not per-def.** It replaced a
  block copy-pasted across the consumer CLI, ""lib/runners\.sh"" and every
  ""defs/*/run\.sh"" — three copies that drifted. A contract test asserts no
  def re-declares any of it.

  The argv is built as pure data and in deterministic order, so it is
  golden-testable; executing it is the caller's concern. That split is what
  makes "what flags does a run actually get" answerable without Docker.
end note

note bottom of RESOLVE
  **A limit is always returned — never unlimited.** The tiers are
  ""clamp(cpus×256, 512, ceiling)"" for a normal image and
  ""clamp(cpus×512, 1024, ceiling)"" for a browser image, which needs more
  because Chromium is process-hungry. An operator override is clamped too
  (floor 256, same ceiling): an unbounded override would re-open the fork-bomb
  the limit exists to contain, so it is honoured but bounded.
end note

note bottom of DETECT
  **Darwin has no ""/proc"", and may have no image to probe.** ""PidMax"" is read
  from the Linux kernel the containers actually run on: ""/proc"" directly on
  Linux, otherwise probed inside the engine's VM via an //already local// image
  (never pulled). On a clean mac both can fail — no ""/proc"", no cached image —
  and the historic ""32768"" fallback is far below what OrbStack, Docker Desktop
  and the other engine VMs really run.

  That mattered as a bug, not a nicety: with ""pid_max = 32768"" the
  ""HostCeiling"" term ""pidMax/64"" evaluates to ""512"", below the browser tier's
  floor of ""1024"", so ""EnsurePidsCapability"" failfasted every
  ""*-browser"" run on an otherwise healthy mac. Darwin therefore falls back to a
  VM-scale default instead of the Linux-historic one.
end note

@enduml
